+# Summary: `plan_retention`
+
+## What changed
+
+- `vaultsnap/retention.py`: implemented `plan_retention(snapshots, policy, now)`. It only plans and never deletes. Helpers `_require_aware` and `_bucket_time` were added. The `Plan` class and the signature are unchanged.
+- `tests/test_retention_adr.py` (new): 12 test cases, listed under Verification. No existing test was edited or deleted.
+- `SUMMARY.md`: this file.
+
+Behaviour implemented, by ADR:
+
+- **ADR-0001 (retention model, the base):**
+  - Inputs: every datetime, including `now`, must be timezone-aware, otherwise `ValueError`. Duplicate ids raise `ValueError`.
+  - Ordering: newest first by `taken_at`. Ties go to the greater id (string comparison).
+  - Empty policy (all five rules off): every snapshot is kept, nothing is pruned.
+  - Set-aside: snapshots with a tag in `protect_tags`, or with `taken_at > now` (strictly; a snapshot at exactly `now` is not set aside). They are always kept, are not counted toward any N, and are never a bucket representative.
+  - `keep_last = N`: the N newest remaining snapshots.
+  - `keep_daily/weekly/monthly/yearly = N`: the N newest buckets that contain at least one remaining snapshot, keeping the newest snapshot of each. Empty periods do not use up N.
+  - Rules are independent; a snapshot kept by any rule is kept.
+  - Output: `keep` and `prune` partition the ids, both newest first.
+- **ADR-0003 (ISO weeks):** the weekly key is `isocalendar()[:2]` (ISO year, ISO week), Monday start. This replaces the Sunday-start weeks of ADR-0001. Monthly and yearly keys stay calendar-based.
+- **ADR-0004 (configurable day boundary):** the bucket time is `taken_at` converted to the fixed offset `tz_offset_minutes`, minus `day_start_hour` hours. All daily/weekly/monthly/yearly keys come from this one bucket time. Ordering, `keep_last`, and the `now` comparison use the real `taken_at`. ADR-0002 (UTC) is superseded and not applied.
+
+## Verification
+
+- `python -m unittest` before my change: 7 errors (all `NotImplementedError`).
+- `python -m unittest -v` after my change (Python 3.14.3): **19 tests, all OK**. That is the 7 original tests plus 12 new ones in `tests/test_retention_adr.py`:
+  - ISO week year boundary (2025-12-28 vs 2025-12-30 with `keep_weekly=2`).
+  - Monthly/yearly keys for 2025-12-30.
+  - `day_start_hour=4` moving a 03:30 snapshot into the previous day, and applying to all rules.
+  - `tz_offset_minutes=600` deciding the local day.
+  - Future snapshot kept and not counted.
+  - Snapshot at exactly `now` not set aside.
+  - Protected snapshot never a representative.
+  - Empty periods not consuming N.
+  - Same `taken_at` tie-break by id.
+  - Naive `now` and duplicate ids rejected.
+
+## Not verified / open points
+
+- I ran only the unittest suite on Python 3.14.3. I did not run a linter or type checker, and did not try other Python versions. The code uses only standard-library datetime APIs.
+- I did not run the nightly job or any real config. There is no CI config in the repo, so I did not check what CI runs beyond `python -m unittest`.
+- I did not test daylight-saving handling. ADR-0004 explicitly has none (fixed offset only), so this is by design.
+- Outside the allowed file set, these need follow-up. I did not change them:
+  - `README.md` has the old retention draft. It should point to `docs/adr/` or be updated.
+  - `CHANGELOG.md` still says retention is "being added" under Unreleased. It should be updated when the feature ships.
+  - `docs/adr/0001-retention-model.md` has an old "Weeks" section and a "Day boundaries" section that defers to a superseded ADR. Both are overridden by ADR-0003 and ADR-0004, as the ADR itself says, but a future reader could be confused.
+- I read the ADRs as the authority, as you asked. Where the ADRs left something unstated, I made a choice: a snapshot with `taken_at` in the future is set aside even if it has no tags, and set-aside snapshots appear in `keep` in newest-first order alongside the others.
